{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts sit above realized prices across most horizons; apply a downward level shift (roughly 2–6 €/t on average, larger when the model is >72).",
    "Downtrend underestimation: in sustained sell-offs the model fails to go low enough and too slowly; increase negative drift / steepen the slope when recent 2–4 week momentum is negative.",
    "Horizon-dependent overshoot: errors grow with lead time; apply a horizon-scaled haircut (small in days 1–5, larger beyond ~day 10–30).",
    "Over-optimistic rebound/mean reversion: after sharp drops the model predicts recovery to low/mid-70s that does not materialize; dampen mean-reversion strength and cap rebound amplitude after drawdowns.",
    "Lag around turning points: forecasts react with delay to regime shifts (especially the March decline); add a momentum/regime filter so forecasts follow recent trend faster and avoid projecting past levels forward."
  ]
}
